# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values before running locally.
# Never commit .env.local to git.

# Production origin, no trailing slash. Used for metadataBase in app/layout.tsx.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.akshitas.com

# Keystatic runs DEV-ONLY with local storage, by decision (GH-9). /studio is the
# prod editor and middleware 404s /keystatic in production. So the Keystatic
# GitHub OAuth vars (KEYSTATIC_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID, KEYSTATIC_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET,
# KEYSTATIC_SECRET) are NOT needed anywhere. Do not re-add them.

# /studio GitHub write boundary (GH-1). All server-side only, never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# Fine-grained PAT scoped to Contents read and write on sinhasagar01/akshita-portfolio ONLY.
# STUDIO_GITHUB_TOKEN=
# Owner login password for the /studio write gate.
# STUDIO_OWNER_PASSWORD=
# 32-byte hex, signs the studio_session cookie. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# STUDIO_SESSION_SECRET=
# Set to "github" to enable the GitHub commit path. Default off = fs/no-op.
# STUDIO_WRITE_MODE=

# WHERE studio reads and writes. Both optional, both default to production.
#
# Every write route no-ops unless STUDIO_WRITE_MODE=github, so github mode is the
# ONLY way to exercise save, create, delete, upload, reorder, publish or discard
# — and with these unset that means the production repo's draft branch. To work
# on studio safely, fork or create a scratch repo, point these at it, and scope
# the PAT above to that repo instead.
#
#   1. Fork sinhasagar01/akshita-portfolio (or create an empty repo and push to it).
#   2. Issue a fine-grained PAT with Contents read and write on THAT repo only.
#   3. Set the three lines below and restart dev.
#
# Nothing else changes: the draft branch, publish-merge, discard and the
# read-split all behave identically, just against your own repo. A dev server in
# github mode pointed at the production repo logs a warning on its first API call.
#
# STUDIO_GITHUB_REPO=your-username/akshita-portfolio
# The branch studio treats as published (compare base + publish merge target).
# STUDIO_BASE_BRANCH=main

# Upstash Redis REST creds from the Upstash console. Server-side only, never
# NEXT_PUBLIC_. Two features share them, with OPPOSITE failure postures.
#
# Durable login throttle (GH-7). Without both vars the throttle falls back to an
# in-memory counter (fine for local dev). Required in prod so it survives cold
# starts and spans serverless instances.
#
# Blog love counter (BS-4a). Without both vars the endpoints answer
# { ok: false, reason: "unavailable" } and the UI shows no number — it never
# falls back to memory, because a count that resets on every cold start is a
# fabricated number, which is worse than none.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# Love counter dedupe secret (BS-4a). Any long random string, e.g.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Visitor IPs are stored only as sha256(secret + ip), truncated, so a love can be
# counted once per visitor without keeping an address. The secret is REQUIRED,
# not optional: a bare sha256 of an IP is enumerable across the whole IPv4 space
# in seconds, so hashing without it would look like privacy and provide none.
# With this unset the love endpoints stay off even when Upstash is configured.
# Changing it resets every visitor's "already loved" marker; the counts survive.
# LOVES_HASH_SECRET=
